## Agent-authored artifact schemas

These are the files YOU (the emitting agent) write. `charter.json` is written once, by
`intake`, and is out of scope for a checkpoint (it is read-only after intake locks it).

**brief** — `outputs/waves/wJJ/briefs/<unit-id>.json`, `schema: "ao.overseer.brief/v1"` — one
per unit you emit, written before the unit dispatches:
- `schema`, `unit_id`, `wave` (int), `ask_ids` (≥1, must cite a real charter `ask_id`),
  `work_item` (pattern `^A\d+/[a-z0-9-]{1,40}$`, e.g. `A2/parser` — the stable loop-detection key)
- `kind` (enum, from the table above), `goal` (≤1500 chars), `acceptance` (≥1, each ≤300 chars)
- `approach_change` (≤600 chars, or `null` — required when this unit re-attempts a work item at
  its attempt cap)
- `prior_attempts` (list of earlier unit ids on this work item), `context_paths`, `touches`

**breadcrumb** — `outputs/progress/<unit-id>.json`, `schema: "ao.overseer.breadcrumb/v1"` —
written by the unit itself, a declared output the engine enforces exists:
- `schema`, `unit_id` (must equal the unit's own id), `outcome` (`done|partial|blocked|no_op`),
  `verdict` (`pass|fail|na`), `summary` (≤300 chars)
- `changed_paths` (≤50, each `"<repo_id>:<rel path>"`), `acceptance_met` (list of ints indexing
  the brief's `acceptance`), `followups` (≤5, each ≤200 chars), `needs_input` (bool)

**verdict** — `outputs/checkpoints/ck-JJ/verdict.json`, `schema: "ao.overseer.verdict/v1"` —
written by the checkpoint (you), once per checkpoint:
- `schema`, `checkpoint` (`ck-JJ`), `stage` (echo of the digest's stage),
  `decision` (`continue|redirect|hold|stabilize|closeout`), `rationale` (≤2000 chars),
  `next_wave_goal` (≤400 chars)
- `alignment` — one entry per charter ask: `{ask_id, status: on_track|at_risk|drifted|met|deferred,
  evidence (≤600 chars), deferred_reason?: out_of_budget|blocked_needs_input|human_descoped|infeasible}`
- `criteria` — one entry per charter acceptance criterion: `{id, status: met|unmet|deferred}`
- `signal_responses` — one entry per digest signal id: `{signal_id, response: redirect|descope|accept|hold, rationale}`
- `hold_questions` (list of strings; required, ≥1, iff `decision == "hold"`)

**final-verify sidecar** — `outputs/final/verify-verdict.json`, `schema: "ao.overseer.final-verify/v1"` —
OPTIONAL, written by `final-verify` after `verify.md`; NOT a declared output (the tail's `outputs`
stay exactly `[verify.md]`) and never a gate; read only by `ao report-usage`:
- `schema`, `asks` — one entry per charter ask: `{id, verdict: met|partial|not_met}`

**hold-request** — `control/hold-request.json`, `schema: "ao.overseer.hold/v1"` — written only
when `decision == "hold"`:
- `schema`, `checkpoint`, `created_at`, `questions` (≥1 strings), `needs_input_path`

## Budget stages and allowed decisions

The digest (`outputs/checkpoints/ck-JJ/digest.json`, tool-written, read-only to you) reports a
`stage` computed from actual **and projected** spend against this run's `converge_pct` /
`stabilize_pct` / `closeout_pct` of `run_budget_usd`. The stage only ever moves forward
(latched) and fixes which decisions your verdict may pick:

| stage | allowed decisions |
|---|---|
| explore | `continue`, `redirect`, `hold`, `stabilize`, `closeout`* |
| converge | `continue`†, `redirect`†, `hold`, `stabilize`, `closeout`* |
| stabilize | `stabilize`, `closeout`, `hold` |
| closeout / `must_close` | `closeout` |

\* `closeout` before the `stabilize` stage is allowed **only** when the ledger already shows,
for every non-deferred ask, a `verify`-kind unit with `verdict: pass` after that ask's last
`implement`/`fix`/`stabilize`/`document` unit (OV-R14) — "done early" must be verified, not
merely claimed.

† In `converge`, `continue`/`redirect` units may only target **existing** work items — no new
scope (OV-R11).

`must_close` (the digest tells you this directly) forces `decision: closeout` regardless of
stage (OV-R16) — it is set once the run's `max_waves`/`max_stabilize_passes` cap is hit, once
stage reaches `closeout`, or once an operator has run `overseer_tool.py request-closeout`.

## Signal types and response rules

The digest's `signals[]` is a deterministic list computed from the ledger, path history, and
previous verdicts. Your verdict's `signal_responses[]` must answer **every** signal id the
digest reports, `response ∈ {redirect, descope, accept, hold}`; `accept` on a `high`-severity
signal requires a `rationale` of at least 40 characters.

| signal | meaning | severity |
|---|---|---|
| `period_repeat` | a work item's kind/verdict sequence is a repeating block (e.g. review-fail, fix-pass, review-fail, fix-pass, or A→B→C→D→A→B→C→D) | high if repeated ≥3 times, else medium |
| `mirror_flipflop` | a work item's sequence mirrors itself (A→B→B→A, A→B→C→C→B→A) | medium |
| `content_oscillation` | a tracked file's content hash returned to an earlier state after changing | high if ≥2 paths or ≥2 returns, else medium |
| `breadcrumb_integrity` | a breadcrumb `changed_paths` entry is malformed, out of its repo root, or names an unknown repo | high |
| `stall` | no work item finished (`outcome: done`, `verdict ∈ {pass, na}`) and criteria-met did not rise, for `stall_waves` consecutive waves | high |
| `repeated_failure` | the same work item's last 2 units both have `verdict: fail` | medium |
| `attempt_cap` | a work item reached `max_attempts_per_item` | high |
| `ask_starvation` | a non-met, non-deferred ask got zero units in the last `stall_waves` waves | medium |
| `blocked_units` | a unit in the last wave reported `outcome: blocked` or `needs_input: true` | medium |
| `prompt_changed` | `prompt.md`'s hash no longer matches the locked charter | high |

## The `overseer_model` rule

This run's `overseer_model` is `"{{ params.overseer_model }}"`. When it is **non-empty**, every
`ck-*` entry you emit (the "Next checkpoint" shape above) must additionally carry
`"model": "{{ params.overseer_model }}"`. When it is empty (the default), omit `model` entirely
and the checkpoint inherits the `manager` agent's own model.

## Hard rules (a violation fails the checkpoint and halts the run)

Every rule below is printed with an `OV-` prefix by the tool, **except** `HOLD`, `BUDGET`, and
`FANOUT`, whose messages start with that literal word verbatim (no `OV-` prefix) so an operator
can `grep` for them directly.

**Currently enforced** (by the shipped `tools/overseer_tool.py`):

- **OV-CFG-0** — `overseer-config.json` is missing, unreadable, not valid JSON, not a JSON
  object, or a required numeric field is not numeric.
- **OV-CFG-1** — `converge_pct` / `stabilize_pct` / `closeout_pct` are not strictly increasing
  between 0 and 100.
- **OV-CFG-2** — `wave_size` or `max_waves` is below 1, or `run_budget_usd` is not positive.
- **OV-CFG-3** — `max_injected_tasks` is too low for this run's own
  `max_waves`/`wave_size`/`max_expanders_per_wave`/`sub_wave_size` math.
- **OV-ST-1** — the run's `state.json` does not exist at the expected path.
- **OV-ST-2** — `state.json` is unreadable, not valid JSON, not an object, or missing a
  required field (`tasks`, a task's `status`/`cumulative_cost_usd`, `injected_tasks[].id`,
  `breaker_overrides`).
- **OV-INT-1** — `charter.json`'s hash, or `prompt.md`'s hash, no longer matches
  `outputs/overseer/charter.lock.json` (the charter was rewritten, or the prompt changed after
  intake locked it).
- **OV-INT-2** — the ledger's last checkpoint decided `hold`, but `control/hold-request.json`
  is missing or unreadable.
- **OV-INT-3** — the ledger's hash chain is broken (a line's `prev_sha256` does not match the
  recomputed hash of the previous line).
- **OV-INT-4** — `control/hold-request.json` exists but the ledger shows no un-answered `hold`
  decision backing it (a forged/stray hold request).
- **OV-BC-1** — a unit's breadcrumb is unreadable/malformed, or its `unit_id` field does not
  match the unit that produced it.
- **OV-BC-2** — a settled unit (any status other than `failed`) has no breadcrumb at all (this
  template's own extension of the `BC-*` bucket, beyond the design doc's normative list —
  fail-closed rather than fabricate data for a unit that should have written its own
  breadcrumb).
- **OV-BR-1** — a unit's brief (`outputs/waves/wJJ/briefs/<unit-id>.json`) is missing,
  unreadable, or not a JSON object.
- **HOLD** — a checkpoint's pre-hook refuses to run because the previous checkpoint decided
  `hold` and no `control/hold-answer.md` newer than `control/hold-request.json` exists yet.
- **BUDGET** — a wave unit's pre-hook refuses to dispatch because spend already meets or
  exceeds the effective budget.
- **FANOUT** — a wave unit's pre-hook refuses to dispatch because the run's injected-task
  count already exceeds `max_injected_tasks`.
- **OV-RUNNING** — `request-closeout` was invoked while the run's `state.json` status is not
  `failed`/`cancelled` (it refuses to race in-flight dispatch).

**Implemented** (the manifest/verdict checker — `intake-check`/`ckpt-check` are real, shipped
subcommands; run them yourself in dry-run mode before you finish, per "Self-check" below.
**Exception**: `expander-check` does NOT exist — `expand`-kind units / `max_expanders_per_wave >
0` are not implemented in this epic; leave `max_expanders_per_wave` at its default `0`):

- **OV-R1** — strict JSON `{"tasks": [...]}`.
- **OV-R2** — field whitelist per entry class.
- **OV-R3** — id patterns, unique, not pre-existing.
- **OV-R4** — units ≤ `allowed_wave_size`.
- **OV-R5** — expanders ≤ `max_expanders_per_wave`, leaves never emit.
- **OV-R6** — brief exists and is valid, `ask_ids ⊆ charter`, `agent` and `instruction` exactly
  as `kind_map[kind]`.
- **OV-R7** — unit outputs exactly [report, breadcrumb], inputs include the brief.
- **OV-R8** — `depends_on` ∋ E (the emitter) and ⊆ {E} ∪ this manifest.
- **OV-R9** — units carry exactly `pre_hook: {"use": "ov-unit-gate"}`, no
  `post_hook`/`model`/`max_turns`, `effort ∈ {low, medium, high}`.
- **OV-R10** — exactly one of {next checkpoint, tail}, exact shapes (`model` on `ck-*` iff
  `overseer_model` is set).
- **OV-R11** — stage restrictions (converge: existing work items only; stabilize:
  stabilize/verify/document only).
- **OV-R12** — the verdict answers every signal id; `accept` on high needs a rationale ≥40
  chars; `deferred` needs `deferred_reason` plus evidence ≥40 chars.
- **OV-R13** — attempt cap (`approach_change` required at the cap; forbidden above cap+1).
- **OV-R13c** — rename dodge: a new work-item whose brief goal is too similar to, or whose
  prior attempts/touches intersect, a capped item's — reuse the capped key with
  `approach_change`, descope it, or hold instead.
- **OV-R14** — decision ↔ manifest consistency, including verified early closeout.
- **OV-R15** — all JSON within 1 MiB, schema version `v1`.
- **OV-R16** — `must_close` ⇒ `closeout`.

## Self-check before you exit

Before you finish, run this template's own checker in dry-run mode and fix every violation it
reports:

```bash
{{ params.python_bin }} {{ workspace_root }}/{{ instance_dir }}/tools/overseer_tool.py \
  ckpt-check --dry-run --task-id <your-checkpoint-id> \
  --workspace-root {{ workspace_root }} --instance-dir {{ instance_dir }}
```

(`intake` runs `intake-check --dry-run` the same way, in place of `ckpt-check`.) Both
subcommands are real and shipped — actually run this before you finish, don't just re-read the
"Hard rules" above by hand. If a `check-result.json` from a previous attempt already exists in
your checkpoint directory
(`{{ instance_dir }}/outputs/checkpoints/<your-checkpoint-id>/check-result.json`), read it
first — it names exactly which rule failed last time.

